            {
                id abi-headers
                title "Decide whether the port ships histedit.h, editline/readline.h and the man page"
                log (
                    {
                        at 2026-08-05T08:51:34.552190783Z
                        author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                        kind @note
                        text "Raised by errata-reconcile, which could not adjudicate two register entries without it.\n\nERR-terminal-64 (doc/editline.3 lists SIGSTOP among the signals libedit handles, which it does not) and most of ERR-core-api-34 (annotation errors in histedit.h) both carry a \"fix the documentation\" disposition. The port ships no histedit.h and nobody has touched doc/editline.3, so neither can be resolved or honestly called reproduced. They sit at open/partial until this is settled.\n\nThe question is not cosmetic. A drop-in replacement is consumed at two layers:\n\n- Link time, which is the .so and its exported symbols. That part is decided and being built.\n- COMPILE time, which is the headers. A consumer that does `#include <histedit.h>` and builds against us needs a header from somewhere. If we do not ship one, we are drop-in only for already-compiled binaries, and any consumer rebuilding from source still needs libedit installed — which rather defeats the purpose for a distribution that wants to replace it outright.\n\nShipping headers pulls in consequences worth deciding deliberately rather than discovering:\n\n- The C's headers carry the annotation defects the register already logs. Shipping them verbatim reproduces those defects at compile time for every consumer; fixing them diverges from the artifact we are replacing. That is the same reproduce-versus-fix fork conformance-policy governs, arriving in a place the policy does not obviously reach, because a header is not runtime behaviour.\n- histedit.h declares el_set/el_get as variadic. Our ABI is fixed-arity per the varargs decision, which is correct on x86-64 SysV and aarch64 AAPCS and wrong on aarch64 Apple. A header we ship is where that would have to be stated.\n- editline/readline.h has the completion_matches const mismatch ERR-readline-53 records, which is only observable to a compiling consumer — so it is a header-only defect that shipping would either propagate or fix.\n\nNot blocking anything today. Worth settling before the register is called reconciled, since two entries are parked on it."
                    }
                )
            }
        )
        log (
            {
                at 2026-08-05T08:39:16.111071698Z
                author "brendan@necessary.nu"
                kind @note
                text "The vis/unvis C surface must NOT be exported. This corrects a conclusion reached during abi-errno, which held that \"the real libedit.so on Linux compiles vis.c/unvis.c and exports the symbols\" and proposed a plan node to export them. Measured on this host, the opposite is true.\n\n`nm -D /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libedit.so.2` defines 205 symbols, none of them vis-related, and carries three UNDEFINED imports:\n\n    U strunvis@LIBBSD_0.0\n    U strvis@LIBBSD_0.0\n    U vis@LIBBSD_0.0\n\nSo Debian's libedit gets vis from libbsd. `src/Makefile.in:97-98` gates the sources on `@HAVE_VIS_FALSE@`/`@HAVE_UNVIS_FALSE@`, and with libbsd present the configure probe succeeds, so vis.c and unvis.c are never compiled in. glibc itself has no strvis — its only 'vis' symbols are madvise/fadvise relatives.\n\nTwo consequences:\n\n1. No plan node is needed for exporting the vis family, and none should be added. The drop-in contract is what libedit.so.2 actually exports, which excludes them.\n\n2. Exporting them anyway would be actively harmful, not merely redundant. Our artifact is installed with libedit.so.0 symlinked onto it. A process that loads both it and libbsd would then have two definitions of strvis in scope, and ELF interposition binds by load order, so libbsd's own callers could silently start calling ours. That is the same interposition hazard ERR-readline-53 already records for `completion_matches`, arriving from the other direction.\n\nWhat remains true from the abi-errno finding: the core's vis/unvis engine records ENOSPC/ENOMEM/EINVAL faithfully, and no C caller can observe those through us because we export no entry point that would. That is correct rather than a gap — the values are observable only through our own history-file path, which is an internal caller.\n\nOne thing this does raise, and it is NOT resolved: our vis/unvis is translated from NetBSD's vis.c, but the history files a Debian user already has on disk were written by libedit calling *libbsd's* vis. If those two disagree on any encoding, we misread existing history files. libbsd's vis derives from NetBSD's so they are probably identical, but \"probably\" is not a basis for a format we must read. Worth a differential test against libbsd before the history file format is declared done."
            }
        )
    }

